# Python Port Plan — Step 10: A Standard Tool Library

## Goal

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/10_standard_tool_library` to
`week1_baseline/python/10_standard_tool_library`. This step adds three
built-in tool libraries that `run()`/`repl()` register automatically:
`Tools::FileSystem` (pwd, list_directory, read_file, write_file,
delete_file, search_files — sandboxed to a working directory),
`Tools::Shell` (run_command, with a timeout and optional allow-list), and
`Tools::Mud` (27 CircleMUD gameplay tools riding on a persistent telnet
session). Version bumps to `0.10.0`.

**Per your confirmed decision**: `FileSystem` and `Shell` get a full,
faithful port (no external dependencies, straightforward). `Tools::Mud`
depends on a bespoke Ruby gem, `mud_manager`, for the actual telnet session
— not something pip-installable. Rather than stub it out, this plan writes
a **real, minimal Python telnet session class** (raw `socket` +
`threading`) that implements the same operations `mud_manager` provides
(open/close/send_command/read_until/read_until_prompt/login), so the MUD
tools are genuinely functional, not fakes. I found the actual
`mud_manager` gem source vendored in this repo at
`week0_explore/mud_manager/lib/mud_manager/{session,primitives}.rb` and
read it in full — this is the authoritative reference for the session and
primitives translation below, in addition to the Ruby step's own
`tools/mud.rb`.

