## Design decisions (confirmed with user)

- **`Tools::Mud`'s dependency is reimplemented, not stubbed.** Write
  `lib/boukensha/mud_session.py` (mirrors `MudManager::Session`) and
  `lib/boukensha/mud_primitives.py` (mirrors `MudManager::Primitives`) as
  real, working code translated directly from the vendored gem source
  above — not placeholder/fake tool bodies. No new pip dependency needed:
  Python's stdlib `socket` + `threading` covers everything `mud_manager`
  uses (Ruby's `Mutex`/`ConditionVariable`/`Thread` map directly to
  Python's `threading.Lock`/`threading.Condition`/`threading.Thread`).

## `MudSession` translation (`lib/boukensha/mud_session.py`)

| Ruby (`MudManager::Session`) | Python (`MudSession`) |
|---|---|
| `Mutex` + `ConditionVariable` guarding `@buffer` | `threading.Condition` (has a built-in lock; use `cond.wait(timeout)`, `cond.notify_all()`) |
| `@socket = TCPSocket.new(@host, @port)` | `socket.create_connection((host, port), timeout=connect_timeout)` |
| Background `Thread` calling `@socket.readpartial(4096)` in a loop | `threading.Thread(target=self._reader_loop, daemon=True)`; loop calls `sock.recv(4096)`, breaks on empty bytes |
| `strip_iac(bytes)` — manual IAC/WILL/WONT/DO/DONT/SB..SE stripper | Direct byte-for-byte port of the same state machine over `bytes` (Python `bytes` objects), same 3-case branching (`IAC IAC` literal, 3-byte negotiation, `SB...IAC SE` variable-length subnegotiation) |
| `send_command(command)` — String, `:return`/`:enter` → `""`, or `.raw`-responding object | `send_command(self, command)` — `str`, the sentinel `None` (or a dedicated `RETURN` marker) → `""`, or a `Command` namedtuple/dataclass with a `.raw` attribute |
| `drain` | Non-blocking: acquire the condition's lock, swap-and-return the buffer |
| `read_until_quiet(quiet_seconds=1.0, timeout=None)` | Same "wait for N quiet seconds since last byte, or overall timeout" loop, using `monotonic()` and `cond.wait_for`/manual deadline math |
| `read_until(pattern, timeout=None)` | `pattern` accepts `str` (escaped) or a compiled `re.Pattern`; same match-then-slice-buffer logic under the condition lock |
| `read_until_prompt(timeout=None)` — waits for `"> "`, falls back to `drain()` with a warning on timeout | Same; `PROMPT_SENTINEL = "> "` |
| `login(username, password)` — the username/password/menu dance | Direct port: `read_until(r"By what name.*\?")`, send username, `read_until(r"Password")`, send password, `read_until(r"Welcome|Reconnecting|Wrong password")`, branch exactly as Ruby does (skip menu on Reconnecting, send blank + `"1"` + `read_until_quiet()` on Welcome, raise `LoginError` on Wrong password) |
| `Session::Error`/`ConnectionError`/`LoginError`/`Timeout` (Ruby exception subclasses) | `MudSessionError`/`MudConnectionError`/`MudLoginError`/`MudTimeoutError` (Python exception subclasses of a common `MudSessionError(Exception)`) |

## `MudPrimitives` translation (`lib/boukensha/mud_primitives.py`)

Stateless — no session/I/O. Each Ruby `module_function` becomes a
module-level Python function with the same name/signature (keyword args
stay keyword args). `Command = Struct.new(:primitive, :raw, :verb, :args, keyword_init: true)`
becomes `@dataclass` `Command` with `primitive: str`, `raw: str`,
`verb: str`, `args: dict[str, Any]`, and `__str__` returning `self.raw`
(mirrors Ruby's `def to_s = raw`). `check_enum!`/`require_str!` port as
private (`_`-prefixed) helper functions raising `ValueError` (Ruby's
`ArgumentError` analog, consistent with `Tasks::Base`'s existing
`ArgumentError` → `ValueError` convention). All the `%w[...]` frozen arrays
(`DIRECTIONS`, `POSITIONS`, `ATTACK_STYLES`, etc.) port as module-level
tuples of strings.

## `Tools::FileSystem`/`Tools::Shell` translation

Straightforward, no external dependencies:

- **Path sandboxing**: `resolve(path)` — `abs = (root / path).resolve()` (or
  manual `os.path.normpath` to match Ruby's `File.expand_path`, which
  doesn't resolve symlinks — prefer `os.path.normpath(os.path.join(root, path))`
  over `Path.resolve()` to avoid symlink-resolution divergence from Ruby);
  reject unless `abs == root or abs.startswith(root + os.sep)`.
- **`search_files`**: compile `pattern` via `re.compile`, catching
  `re.error` the way Ruby catches a bad `Regexp.new`; walk files matching
  `glob` (Python `pathlib.Path.rglob`/`glob.glob`) under the resolved
  search root, format matches as `f"{path}:{lineno}:{line}"`.
- **`run_command`**: Ruby's `Open3.capture2e(command, chdir: root)` runs
  the command through a shell with combined stdout+stderr. Python:
  `subprocess.run(command, shell=True, cwd=root, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)`,
  catching `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` → `"command timed out after {timeout}s"`
  and `FileNotFoundError`/`OSError` → `"command not found"` (note: with
  `shell=True`, a genuinely missing command usually surfaces as a nonzero
  exit + shell's own "command not found" text on stdout/stderr rather than
  a Python exception — mirror Ruby's `Errno::ENOENT` case as best-effort,
  test against a real missing command to confirm which path actually
  triggers).

